According to excerpt [2] (paper: 1911.04263, section: B. Dueling DQN Agent), Dueling DQN modifies the architecture of Double DQN (DDQN) by decoupling the single stream into a state-value stream and an action-advantage stream. This is achieved by adding a batch normalization layer to the input layer and modifying the number of neurons in the hidden layer according to the dimensions of inputs and outputs.

Excerpt [2] also mentions that Dueling DQN uses three important techniques in DQN, including:

(1) an experience replay buffer that allows the agent to be trained off-policy and decouples the strong correlations between the consecutive training data;
(2) importance sampling is used to increase the algorithm learning efficiency and final policy quality;
(3) adoption of a DDQN structure, which fixes the q-targets periodically, and then stabilizes the agent updates.

These modifications aim to improve the Q-value approximation and overall performance of the agent.
